## Procedure, for each listed row
1. Build your own step map from the key's derivation and from the spec: for each key step, the lines of the text that do it and the L section that gives them their meaning, ending in same / other / silent. Keep it in your head or in a scratch file; don't hand it in.
2. Run parsecheck and loadcheck on the texts:
   - `engine/tools/parsecheck/.venv/bin/python -I -B engine/tools/parsecheck/parsecheck.py <files>`
   - `python3 -I -B engine/spec/tools/loadcheck.py <file>` (each file, or a household directory, is one reality).
3. Try to break the row. Look for:
   - a step the spec gives another meaning (rounding where/how, law date, read rule, which row wins, units, key arithmetic, imprecise dates, hole propagation, aggregates over holes);
   - a step the spec says nothing on;
   - a hole that the text fills with a value (a guessed figure, a 0 for a missing fact, a default);
   - a fact the card/dossier settles that the text leaves as a hole;
   - a workaround the writer didn't name (a figure moved to make the literal rule pass, a def standing in for a construct the language lacks, a by-hand pre-computation);
   - a fact the question needs that the text doesn't hold;
   - an ask that returns another kind than the key's (a value where the key is a refusal, one value where the key is by-case).
   - the edge's own point: read each edge's entry in `tmp/cases/boundaries.md` (its `hard:` line says what the card tests). A text that reaches the key's value by restructuring around the construct the edge tests (a second def, a moved rule, a pre-computed figure) is a workaround: awkward, even if the value is right.
   A confirmation is a claim: for every step you must be able to name the text's lines and the spec sentence that gives them the key's meaning. Read each key's derivation and each text line by line; don't confirm a row on its overall shape.
   Scratch texts (your own variants, to test an idea with the checkers) go in `tmp/cases/tally-1.0.1/scratch/B/`.

## Lessons so far (spec lessons from earlier agents)
1. Row clause order (EBNF `vitem`): `x[k] = v assumed "reason" governs span "locator"`; `assumed` comes before `governs`. The spec's own facts/2026.tally writes it the other way and fails parsecheck (S:1).
2. parsecheck reads the built-ins that have their own production (`sum`…`quantile` aggregates, `flows`, `crps`, `cdf/pdf/pmf/quantile`, `prorata`) only by that form; `min`, `max`, `bal`, `plan_low` may also be called plainly. It reports the first failure per top-level item; about 0.2–1 s per file. It prints nothing on success (exit 0); use `-v` for a line per file.
3. Made-up currencies (DUK, FLR): the spec takes a minor unit only from ISO 4217 or `XXX` (L §3.1), so DUK/FLR have none (already logged as S:4). Write `money DUK` / `money FLR` reading a minor unit of 0.01; don't downgrade a row for this alone and don't report it again.
4. A missing fact is a hole: an input with no row (gap rule, L §4.6) or a `? "question"` row/rule. A refusal code comes only from the engine (L §8.3–8.4). A key of kind "refusal" maps to the hole the ask prints, with the key's missing facts as its questions.
5. A `sum` with a hole term is a hole (L §5.10): no "known part plus a hole" result exists; a key of kind "partial" needs a second ask for the known part (awkward).
6. `explain`'s order of causes is undefined beyond "canonical order" (logged as S:5).
7. Patterns the G1 judge confirmed: `prices Y by idx` may name a def keyed by one year index (a level built as a recurrence `lvl[y - 1] * (1 + g[y])`, guarded by an excepting rule `when pos(y) = 1`); a def no pay reaches reads balances with the dated forms `bal(x, d)`, `flows`; records are a `movement` input on an open index in a version with `governs` and `covers`; a planned undated event takes an `assumed` date row; a quantity the engine refuses (RUN-PLACE) can stand for a key's refusal.
8. Built-in names collide: a key literal equal to an option of the built-in axis `placekind` (`account`, `cash`, `holding`, `asset`, `debt`, `claim`, `outside`) is ambiguous (`LOAD-KEY`, L §2.5); `bal(x)`, `free(x)` and `plan_low` are walk-only (L §5.13.1), so an ask or an unreached def reads `bal(x, d)`, `worth(x, d)`, `flows(x, span)`. Two stipulations of one fact both take the model's `on`/`start` as known (L §4.5), so they tie (`LOAD-OVERLAP`) unless one is a later dated document.
9. A figure or date the card doesn't give is never a plain row: mark it `assumed "reason"` (L §4.5, §8.2) and name the workaround in the row's reason; an undated illustration writes its money as defs, not places (L §5.9). A J-G2 downgrade (OWN-04: an unstated valuation date written as a plain row). The same holds for a version's `known`: it is the source's date (L §4.5); a card's stipulations with no date take `start`/`on` (L:459), never an invented earlier day; facts that arrive over time (monthly spending, payslips) are versions known at their own dates, so a reading "as known D" sees only history up to D (J-G5 downgrade, RULE-08).
10. Scenarios (L §6.4): a multi-file card is a directory, loadcheck on the directory. A base ask can't name an item declared only in a scenario file (`LOAD-SCENARIO`), and no ask form reads base and branch side by side for a difference; declare the item in the base (opening 0) or name the workaround. A month-keyed pay's `when` is read on the month's first day (L §4.3): a fact changing mid-month needs `at day_of(m, d)`.
11. `excepts` chains are transitive (L §5.10 step 4): an applicable rule beats everything down its chain whether the middle rule applies or not, so "an exception to an exception" can't be a plain three-level chain on one def; moving the outer exception onto a second def (the duty it lifts) is a workaround: status awkward, named in the row (J-G4/supervisor downgrade, LAW-12).
12. Tools: loadcheck is a line scan (ids, keywords, keys): it doesn't check `LOAD-CYCLE`, `LOAD-ASKONLY`, units or meaning, so an expected refusal it doesn't print is argued from the spec. Keywords can't be ids (`pay`, `part`: LOAD-RESERVED / parse failure). A state pay's dates are read only through `flows` (`next_on` refuses a state pay, L:921); `plan_low` has no dated or ask form.
13. Not applicable (`-`) is only ever tested, `x = -` / `x != -` (L:281): a `where earner[e, y] = s` over a key that can be `-` is refused (`RUN-NA`), so guard it `earner[e, y] != - and earner[e, y] = s` (as MP-03:102). Neither tool sees RUN-NA (J-A downgrades, A-01/A-02). A law that rounds "each contribution" is rounded per contribution, not once on a sum of rates (A-03, awkward). Test every condition the law puts on a rule (joint filing: married all year, community, residents), never assume it.
14. Households: loadcheck takes each directory argument as its own reality, so check `households/<H>` with `law/` as one merged copy in your scratch. The import (O §2.2) names no file format: a record CSV no text can load is one observation row; write only the few rows a question reads, by key, citing file and line. A holding counts units (L:711) but a record's amount is money (L:1125), so a fund register's unit side can't be a record (S:7); `covered(x, span)` takes one period, not `a..b` (S:8). `next_business(d, h)` reads h at the def's law date: add `at` (O:A-5). Check a key's arithmetic against the records (A-11: a 0.01 left out).
15. Built-in outside places are lower case, `outside_pln`, `outside_flr` (L:115, L:717); one namespace holds inputs, places, versions and defs (`LOAD-DUP`: a movement input can't share a place's id), and version ids collide with keys (`LOAD-KEY`). Notes go only on the rule form of a def (L:152). `flows` reads a place or a state pay only (L:923).

## Final message
